WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.018
Take the chain of springs

2
00:00:05.099 --> 00:00:07.869
and let the springs grow in number

3
00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:09.661
and shrink in length.

4
00:00:09.661 --> 00:00:13.082
In the limit, it is an elastic bar on the

5
00:00:13.164 --> 00:00:15.282
interval from zero to one

6
00:00:15.282 --> 00:00:18.540
fixed at both ends, with a load f of x.

7
00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:21.582
We will approximate it with

8
00:00:21.682 --> 00:00:23.576
a hundred unknowns.

9
00:00:23.576 --> 00:00:27.265
The matrix has ten thousand entries.

10
00:00:27.265 --> 00:00:30.355
How many of them are not zero?

11
00:00:30.355 --> 00:00:32.050
Pause and guess.

12
00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:38.720
The three steps survive the limit.

13
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.809
The stretch is the slope u prime

14
00:00:41.809 --> 00:00:44.717
the tension is c times u prime

15
00:00:44.808 --> 00:00:48.170
and balance says minus the derivative

16
00:00:48.261 --> 00:00:50.260
of c u prime equals f.

17
00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.350
Minus d by d x plays A transpose.

18
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.262
The energy keeps its form

19
00:00:56.262 --> 00:00:59.513
with sums turned into integrals

20
00:00:59.513 --> 00:01:03.355
one half c u prime squared, minus f u.

21
00:01:03.453 --> 00:01:06.902
The solution of the equation is the

22
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.660
function that minimises it.

23
00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:12.304
With c and f equal to one

24
00:01:12.393 --> 00:01:15.217
the solution is the parabola one

25
00:01:15.305 --> 00:01:17.511
half x times one minus x

26
00:01:17.511 --> 00:01:21.306
and its energy is minus one twenty fourth.

27
00:01:21.394 --> 00:01:25.277
The best multiple of a single sine wave gets

28
00:01:25.365 --> 00:01:28.895
minus zero point zero four one zero six

29
00:01:28.895 --> 00:01:32.690
just above, as the minimum principle says.

30
00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:36.082
We cannot search all functions

31
00:01:36.082 --> 00:01:39.529
so we search a subspace, as Ritz did.

32
00:01:39.529 --> 00:01:43.520
Split the interval into pieces of length h.

33
00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:47.238
The hat function phi i is one at node i

34
00:01:47.238 --> 00:01:49.597
zero at every other node

35
00:01:49.597 --> 00:01:52.590
and straight in between: a tent.

36
00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:56.797
Combinations of hats are exactly the broken

37
00:01:56.887 --> 00:01:59.848
lines through chosen heights U i

38
00:01:59.848 --> 00:02:02.180
which vanish at the ends.

39
00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:05.637
Putting a broken line into the energy

40
00:02:05.721 --> 00:02:08.661
gives the same kind of P as before

41
00:02:08.745 --> 00:02:10.676
with a stiffness matrix

42
00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:12.608
K and a load vector F.

43
00:02:12.692 --> 00:02:15.379
K i j is the integral of c times

44
00:02:15.463 --> 00:02:17.395
the slopes of two hats

45
00:02:17.395 --> 00:02:21.090
and F i is the integral of f times one hat.

46
00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:24.405
A hat's slope is plus or minus

47
00:02:24.496 --> 00:02:27.139
one over h on its two pieces

48
00:02:27.139 --> 00:02:28.963
and zero elsewhere.

49
00:02:29.054 --> 00:02:31.241
Two hats overlap only if

50
00:02:31.333 --> 00:02:35.070
they are neighbours, so K is tridiagonal.

51
00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:37.487
On one piece, an element

52
00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:39.968
the two hats there contribute

53
00:02:40.051 --> 00:02:43.441
c over h times one, minus one, minus one

54
00:02:43.441 --> 00:02:43.937
one.

55
00:02:43.937 --> 00:02:46.996
That is the matrix of a single spring

56
00:02:47.079 --> 00:02:49.064
with stiffness c over h.

57
00:02:49.146 --> 00:02:51.214
Adding up the elements is

58
00:02:51.296 --> 00:02:52.867
assembling springs

59
00:02:52.867 --> 00:02:55.100
every element is a spring.

60
00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:59.681
With three interior nodes, h is one quarter.

61
00:02:59.777 --> 00:03:02.278
K is four times the second

62
00:03:02.374 --> 00:03:04.105
difference matrix

63
00:03:04.105 --> 00:03:07.567
and each load entry is one quarter

64
00:03:07.567 --> 00:03:09.490
the area of a tent.

65
00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.683
The solution is three thirty seconds

66
00:03:12.683 --> 00:03:15.295
one eighth, three thirty seconds

67
00:03:15.295 --> 00:03:17.984
exactly the parabola at the nodes.

68
00:03:17.984 --> 00:03:19.367
In one dimension

69
00:03:19.367 --> 00:03:22.440
that is a special gift of this problem.

70
00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:24.615
Between the nodes

71
00:03:24.615 --> 00:03:28.336
a straight line cannot follow a parabola.

72
00:03:28.336 --> 00:03:29.754
On one element

73
00:03:29.754 --> 00:03:32.767
the gap is one half of x minus a

74
00:03:32.767 --> 00:03:36.311
times b minus x, largest in the middle

75
00:03:36.311 --> 00:03:38.260
h squared over eight.

76
00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:42.539
With one, three, seven and fifteen nodes

77
00:03:42.635 --> 00:03:45.030
the largest error is zero

78
00:03:45.126 --> 00:03:47.139
point zero three one

79
00:03:47.139 --> 00:03:50.876
then zero point zero zero seven eight

80
00:03:50.876 --> 00:03:51.930
and so on.

81
00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:54.376
Each time h is halved

82
00:03:54.376 --> 00:03:57.235
the error is divided by four.

83
00:03:57.235 --> 00:04:00.760
The method is second order accurate.

84
00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:03.491
Back to the opening question.

85
00:04:03.491 --> 00:04:06.310
Pushing anywhere moves the whole bar

86
00:04:06.384 --> 00:04:08.312
so surely every unknown is

87
00:04:08.386 --> 00:04:11.205
coupled to every other, and K is full?

88
00:04:11.205 --> 00:04:12.540
Pause and decide.

89
00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.058
With ninety nine unknowns

90
00:04:18.132 --> 00:04:20.666
K has only two hundred ninety five

91
00:04:20.741 --> 00:04:23.424
nonzero entries out of nine thousand

92
00:04:23.499 --> 00:04:24.840
eight hundred one.

93
00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:27.603
The whole bar responds through

94
00:04:27.683 --> 00:04:29.051
the inverse of K

95
00:04:29.051 --> 00:04:32.026
and that inverse is completely full.

96
00:04:32.026 --> 00:04:35.485
The matrix itself only couples neighbours.

97
00:04:35.565 --> 00:04:38.219
That is why finite elements scale

98
00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:40.230
to millions of unknowns.

99
00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:43.064
And K is positive definite

100
00:04:43.064 --> 00:04:45.732
as a sum of springs must be

101
00:04:45.732 --> 00:04:47.296
for three nodes

102
00:04:47.296 --> 00:04:51.160
its eigenvalues are two point three four

103
00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.380
eight, and thirteen point six six.

104
00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:58.109
Now make the bar from two materials.

105
00:04:58.109 --> 00:05:01.957
The left half is soft, with c equal to one.

106
00:05:01.957 --> 00:05:05.280
The right half is four times stiffer.

107
00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:09.443
The element springs are four, four, sixteen

108
00:05:09.443 --> 00:05:10.223
sixteen.

109
00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:12.737
The middle node sits between

110
00:05:12.823 --> 00:05:15.163
a soft and a stiff element

111
00:05:15.163 --> 00:05:18.977
so its diagonal entry is four plus sixteen

112
00:05:18.977 --> 00:05:19.670
twenty.

113
00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:23.181
The solution sags more on the soft side

114
00:05:23.181 --> 00:05:26.580
and it is still exact at every node point.

115
00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:30.333
The true solution has a kink in the middle.

116
00:05:30.333 --> 00:05:33.261
The tension c u prime is continuous

117
00:05:33.261 --> 00:05:36.584
so where c jumps to four times its value

118
00:05:36.584 --> 00:05:39.671
the slope shrinks by a factor of four.

119
00:05:39.671 --> 00:05:41.649
With a node at the jump

120
00:05:41.649 --> 00:05:45.210
the elements handle that with no extra work.

121
00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:49.377
A differential equation has an energy.

122
00:05:49.472 --> 00:05:52.776
Hat functions turn its minimisation

123
00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:54.569
into K U equals F

124
00:05:54.569 --> 00:05:57.590
where each element is a spring.

125
00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:01.301
K is sparse and positive definite

126
00:06:01.301 --> 00:06:04.860
and the error falls like h squared.

127
00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:07.486
In two and three dimensions

128
00:06:07.567 --> 00:06:11.063
the same assembly of small element matrices

129
00:06:11.144 --> 00:06:13.746
runs the simulations of bridges

130
00:06:13.746 --> 00:06:15.290
engines and bones.
